<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Qin dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">221 BCE – 206 BCE · capital Xianyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">China's first imperial dynasty, which unified the warring states in 221 BCE and imposed one script, one currency and one bureaucracy, then collapsed within fifteen years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Between 230 and 221 BCE King <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">Zheng</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> conquered the six rival states, the last being the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a>. He took the new title Huangdi, emperor, and is known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>, the First Emperor. His capital remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> in the Wei valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a>, the emperor abolished hereditary fiefs and divided the empire into 36, later more, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> run by appointed officials. Script, weights, measures, coinage and axle widths were standardised, roads and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingqu">Lingqu</a> canal were built, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-tian">Meng Tian</a> linked earlier walls against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Harsh Legalist law, mass labour levies and the burning of books in 213 BCE bred resentment. The emperor's mausoleum was guarded by the Terracotta Army.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">First Emperor</a> died in 210 BCE, Zhao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> installed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-er-shi">Huhai</a> as Second Emperor. Revolts led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> began in 209 BCE, and in 206 BCE the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziying">Ziying</a>, surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> kept the Qin system of centralised imperial government.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qin-dynasty.jpg" alt="Qin dynasty" width="320" height="384"><figcaption>Image: Ian Remsen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_dynasty_territory.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>221 BCE – 206 BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xianyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qin Shi Huang, Qin Er Shi, Ziying</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qin Empire</dd></dl></aside></div>
